{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.QBetaOverE0ClassicalVanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.QBetaOverE0ClassicalVanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Units.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.qbeta_over_e0_at_one_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.qbeta_over_e0_at_one_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing.lean#L66-L86","doc":"**$S(q) = Q_\\beta(q) / E_0(q)$ vanishes at the classical point $q = 1$.**\n\nDirect consequence of the numerator-vanishing identity: at `q = 1`\nthe rational function `S_N0` evaluates to `0 / 2 = 0`.\n\n*Consequence for the precision ladder.*  The shape-collapse audit\n`2026-06-03-qbeta-markov-trace-shape-collapse.md` states the `c_k`\nTaylor coefficients come from this `S(q)` — but `S(1) = 0` means\nthe Taylor expansion of `S/S(1)` is degenerate (`0/0`).  Hence the\n`c_k` ladder normalization is not `Q_β / E_0` (which is the\n*SDP-constraint scalar*, evaluated at `q_0`); the actual ladder\nnormalization is `Q_β / [Q_A · cos(α_eff)]` per\n`qbeta_1ppq_achieved.py`, and `Q_A` is the CODATA-free bare scale\nidentified by the SDP strict-complementarity route in PR #1752. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.S_N0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.S_N0","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing.lean#L59-L64","doc":"The simplified closed form of `S(q) = Q_β(q) / E_0(q)` using the\n`N = 0` form of `E_0`.  The `m_e` mass scale cancels in the ratio,\nconsequently the result is a pure rational function of `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.qbeta_over_e0_numerator_at_one_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.OverE0ClassicalVanishing.qbeta_over_e0_numerator_at_one_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing.lean#L46-L57","doc":"**Numerator of $S(q) = Q_\\beta(q)/E_0(q)$ vanishes at $q = 1$.**\n\nThe simplified ratio of the substrate closed forms factors as\n`S(q) = (2q + 1 − q²) · (q² − 1) / (2q²)`.  At `q = 1` the second\nfactor `q² − 1` is zero, so the whole numerator vanishes — an\nalgebraic identity over any commutative ring.  This is the\ndiagnostic of PR #1769: Q_β's `(q + 1)` factor meets E_0's\n`1/[q(q − 1)]` pole and their product carries no constant term\nat the classical point. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing","label":"prop:q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-over-e0-classical-vanishing.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}